 /*
- * SWEET: USB bring-up, step 1 - read-only state probe.
+ * lavender (Redmi Note 7, SDM660): kernel log over USB.
  *
- * The phone's USB is a Synopsys DWC3 (USB 2.0 only, maximum-speed
- * high-speed) at 0x0a600000 behind Qualcomm's dwc-usb3-msm wrapper, with a
- * QUSB2 PHY at 0x088e2000; its power domain is the USB30_PRIM GDSC and its
- * clocks come from GCC (offsets from Linux gcc-sdmmagpie.c). ABL uses this
- * controller for fastboot; this probe only reports what state it is left
- * in after `fastboot boot`. It never writes a register, and it touches the
- * DWC3 / PHY register blocks only when the GCC status registers say their
- * power domain and clocks are on (an access to a gated block would hang).
+ * Replaces the base series' CDC-ECM function with one vendor interface that Windows binds to
+ * WinUSB by itself (Microsoft OS 2.0 descriptors), so the PC needs no driver:
+ *   bulk IN  0x81  the console log ring, from the first boot message on
+ *   bulk OUT 0x01  one text command per packet: "lv:ping", "lv:reset" (boots the installed
+ *                  system), "lv:fastboot" (reboots into the bootloader)
+ * The PC side is tools/usblog.py.
+ *
+ * The controller is the Synopsys DWC3 at 0x0a800000 that ABL has just used for fastboot: it is
+ * powered, clocked, in device mode and halted, with the PHY set up. Only the device controller is
+ * restarted (soft reset, our event buffer, endpoint 0, run). Events are polled from a kernel
+ * thread. Register use follows the DWC3 databook as in Linux drivers/usb/dwc3 (gadget.c, ep0.c)
+ * and the base series' driver for other Qualcomm phones.
  */
